      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>2.1 descriptive attacks – Characterising or describing women in a derogatory manner. This could include, but not limited to: negative generalisations about women’s abilities, appearance, sexual behaviour, intellect, character, or morals.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes hypersexualise women and frame their bodies as objects for men’s pleasure and spectacle. Gendered double standards treat women’s bodies as sexualised and indecent compared with men’s, subject them to censorship on online platforms, and demand specific beauty standards while labelling natural bodily states and diversity as lewd. Women’s and girls’ clothing is policed as distracting to men and boys, while sexual violence survivors and those who report sexual violence face blame, stigma, and characterisations as promiscuous, liars, or intent on harming the perpetrator.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
