      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>2.1 descriptive attacks – Characterising or describing women in a derogatory manner. This could include, but not limited to: negative generalisations about women’s abilities, appearance, sexual behaviour, intellect, character, or morals.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Women are hypersexualised and represented as existing for men’s pleasure and spectacle. Their bodies are treated as sexualised and indecent compared with men’s, expected to meet specific beauty standards while natural bodily states such as menstruation, body hair, breastfeeding, and diverse body shapes and skin colours are considered lewd. Women and girls’ clothing is policed through the idea that certain styles are distracting to men and boys. Survivors who report sexual violence are stigmatised and shamed as promiscuous, liars, or out to get the perpetrator.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
