      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.1 casual use of gendered slurs, profanities, and insults – Using gendered slurs, gender-based profanities and insults, but not to intentionally attack women. Only terms that traditionally describe women are in scope (e.g. ‘b*tch’, ‘sl*t’).</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Online gender-based hate speech frameworks sometimes treat calumny, defamation and insults as a distinct type of conduct, separate from incitement to violence, hatred, discrimination or commission of crime. The legal components attached to these offences vary: some frameworks require gender grounds, intentional act, effects on the victim or repetition over time, while others do not attach these conditions to insults.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
