    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID102",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_36c569ce10848e625e4917ec__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles\n\nMen as tough, unfeeling, aggressive, and expected to conceal or suppress emotional vulnerability - ‘stiff upper lip’ culture.\n\nMen as dominant in the family, within intimate relationships and in social settings with women.\n\nMen as the sexual initiator or entitled to sex with women. Some groups of men in minority groups as more sexually violent and more controlling (based for example on income levels, regions, ethnicity, and/ or religious affiliation).\n\nCulture of acceptance or victim-blaming around rape, especially framed in the light that if the survivor 'had been drinking alcohol', 'wearing clothes considered provocative', 'walking home alone at night', or was ‘leading’ the perpetrator ‘on’.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes portray men as tough, unfeeling, aggressive, and expected to suppress emotional vulnerability, as dominant in families, intimate relationships, and social settings with women, and as sexual initiators or entitled to sex. Some stereotypes further depict men in minority groups as more sexually violent and controlling based on income, region, ethnicity, or religion. A culture of acceptance or victim-blaming around rape frames survivors as responsible if they had been drinking alcohol, wore clothes considered provocative, walked home alone at night, or were perceived as leading the perpetrator on.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_36c569ce10848e625e4917ec__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
