      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes – Asserting immutable, natural or otherwise essential differences between men and women. In some cases, this could be in the form of using women’s traits to attack men. Most sexist jokes will fall into this category.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes portray men as tough, unfeeling, aggressive, and expected to suppress emotional vulnerability, as dominant in families, intimate relationships, and social settings with women, and as sexual initiators or entitled to sex. Some stereotypes further depict men in minority groups as more sexually violent and controlling based on income, region, ethnicity, or religion. A culture of acceptance or victim-blaming around rape frames survivors as responsible if they had been drinking alcohol, wore clothes considered provocative, walked home alone at night, or were perceived as leading the perpetrator on.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "answer_relevance_card_36c569ce10848e625e4917ec__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
